-- <crypt.h> for the standalone profile, after musl 1.2.5 src/crypt/:
--   crypt, crypt_r (<crypt.h>; crypt also in <unistd.h>), encrypt
--   (<unistd.h>) and setkey (<stdlib.h>).
--
-- The schemes and their settings, as musl reads them:
--   $1$salt$             MD5 (crypt_md5.c): at most 8 salt characters, the
--                        closing $ optional; keys over 30000 bytes fail.
--   $5$[rounds=N$]salt$  SHA-256, $6$ SHA-512 (crypt_sha256.c, crypt_sha512.c):
--                        at most 16 salt characters, '\n' and ':' in the salt
--                        fail; rounds= must be decimal digits followed by $, N
--                        below 1000 is 1000 and above 9999999 fails; the
--                        output spells the rounds out when the setting had
--                        rounds= (so "rounds=00001234$" gives rounds=1234$);
--                        keys over 256 bytes fail.
--   $2a$, $2b$, $2x$, $2y$ bcrypt (crypt_blowfish.c): two cost digits, 04
--                        to 19 (00 to 03 and anything above 19 fail), and
--                        22 salt characters; $2x$ reproduces the sign-extension
--                        bug of old crypt_blowfish, $2a$ adds its
--                        countermeasure, $2b$ and $2y$ are the corrected
--                        algorithm. The last salt character is normalised as
--                        musl does (only its top two bits are kept).
--   _CCCCSSSS            BSDi extended DES (crypt_des.c): 4 characters of
--                        count (0 fails) and 4 of salt, each from ./0-9A-Za-z;
--                        the whole key is folded in.
--   anything else        traditional DES: two salt characters (NUL, '\n' or
--                        ':' fail; other characters are mapped as UFC-crypt
--                        maps them with a signed char), the first 8 bytes of
--                        the key.
-- A setting is dispatched as in crypt_r.c: "$1$", "$2?$", "$5$" and "$6$"
-- prefixes, everything else (including "$2$...") is DES.
--
-- Failure: "*" for MD5, SHA and bcrypt; DES gives "*", or "x" when the
-- setting starts with '*', so that the result never equals the setting.
-- Every scheme runs its self-test after the requested hash, as musl does: a
-- wrong build gives "*" instead of a wrong hash, and the second computation
-- overwrites the stack the first one used. bcrypt's key setup is musl's
-- branch-free code (fixed-cost bit operations, no table lookups indexed by
-- password bytes); as in musl, the length of the password and the S-box
-- lookups of the cipher itself are not hidden.
--
-- crypt uses a static 128-byte buffer (not thread-safe, as in musl); crypt_r
-- writes its result into the struct crypt_data it is given and ignores
-- `initialized`. setkey and encrypt keep the key schedule in static storage.
## pragmas.nogc = true
## pragmas.noentrypoint = true

